' --- manual: everything below this line is hand-authored; the script preserves it ---

' The automated pass reads a namespace of data types and nothing else. Almost
' the whole surface of this component is one free function, which the pass
' cannot see, so its output is an empty namespace and the classes below carry
' the entire diagram.
'
' They use fully-qualified class names rather than a reopened namespace block,
' because this PlantUML version crashes when a namespace that already holds
' classes is declared a second time.

class ores::iam::client::make_service_token_provider {
    {static} +make_service_token_provider(nats : ores::nats::service::client&, username : string, password : string, refresh_margin : chrono::seconds = 120) : ores::nats::service::nats_client::token_provider
}
note right of "ores::iam::client::make_service_token_provider"
The component's only exported symbol and the only declaration in its only
public header, service_token_provider.hpp. It is a free function, so the
automated pass draws nothing for it. username is the service account name,
which is also the database user name, and password is that account's plaintext
database password.
end note
note bottom of "ores::iam::client::make_service_token_provider"
It returns a token_provider, not a class of its own: a caller injects the
result into a nats_client, which is how a service account reaches
IAM-authenticated subjects. refresh_margin defaults to 120 seconds, but the
provider replaces it after the first login; see the token_state note below.
end note

class ores::nats::service::nats_client::token_provider {
    +operator()(force : bool) : string
}
note right of "ores::nats::service::nats_client::token_provider"
An alias, not a class: nats_client declares
using token_provider = std::function<std::string(bool force)>. Called with
force = false it returns the cached JWT, refreshing first when the token is
near expiry; called with force = true it re-authenticates unconditionally. It
belongs to ores.nats, and this component's only job is to supply one.
end note

class ores::iam::client::token_state {
    -nats : ores::nats::service::client&
    -username : string
    -password : string
    -refresh_margin : chrono::seconds
    -jwt : string
    -expires_at : chrono::system_clock::time_point
    -access_lifetime_s : int
    +authenticate()
    +refresh()
    +refresh_if_needed()
}
note right of "ores::iam::client::token_state"
Declared in an anonymous namespace in service_token_provider.cpp, so it is
internal to that translation unit and appears in no header. It is the state the
returned callable captures in a shared_ptr, and it carries the behaviour the
header documents: authenticate() posts iam.v1.auth.service-login and caches the
JWT with its expiry, refresh() posts iam.v1.auth.refresh carrying the cached
JWT as an Authorization bearer header, and refresh_if_needed() calls refresh()
once now is within refresh_margin of expires_at.
end note
note bottom of "ores::iam::client::token_state"
The free function calls authenticate() once before it returns, so the first
authenticated request pays no login round-trip and a startup failure surfaces
immediately. authenticate() retries a transient failure with exponential
backoff from 500 ms to a 30 s cap, which covers the race where IAM has not yet
subscribed; a credential failure is thrown at once and never retried. A
successful login also overwrites refresh_margin with one fifth of the
access_lifetime_s the response reports (that member defaults to 1800), so the
caller's margin applies only until the first login and the token is thereafter
renewed at about 80% of its lifetime. A refresh that fails logs a warning and
falls back to authenticate().
end note

class ores::iam::messaging::service_login_request <<struct>> {
    {static} +nats_subject : string_view
}
note right of "ores::iam::messaging::service_login_request"
The subject the provider's first login goes to. The source never spells the
string: it takes service_login_request::nats_subject, so a rename in the
protocol model moves this component's target with it. The value is
"iam.v1.auth.service-login".
end note

class ores::iam::messaging::refresh_request <<struct>> {
    {static} +nats_subject : string_view
}
note right of "ores::iam::messaging::refresh_request"
The subject a proactive refresh goes to, taken the same way from
refresh_request::nats_subject. The value is "iam.v1.auth.refresh".
end note

class ores::nats::service::client
note right of "ores::nats::service::client"
The raw connected NATS client. The header states the lifetime rule in the
@param nats line: it must outlive the provider. The provider stores a
reference, not a copy, and uses it for both the login and the refresh request.
end note

